CONC = [
("h1", "Conclusion"),
("p", "The volume began with a plain statement: Paul names several things the Cross did, each against a different problem, and the passages are not rival theories and not one doctrine said again. It has tried to show that from the text. In Romans 3 God sets Christ forth as a propitiation to declare his righteousness. In Romans 4 and 5 Christ is delivered for offences and dies for the ungodly, and enemies are reconciled by the death of the Son. In Galatians 3 Christ is made a curse for us. In 2 Corinthians 5 one dies for all, and God makes him to be sin for us. In Romans 6 and Colossians 2 and 3 the believer is crucified and dead with Christ. In Colossians 2 the handwriting is blotted out and the powers are spoiled. In Ephesians 2 and Colossians 1 the blood and the cross make peace."),
("p", "The passages share a subject, a few words and the language of grace, and they differ in the problem, the means and the result. The volume has asked of each the same four questions, and has not carried the answer of one passage into another. It has also said where each passage stops. None of them explains how the death does its work; none states a doctrine of extent beyond its own clauses; none tells what earlier believers were saved by; none makes the Body a party to a covenant of Israel’s."),
("ctbl", "The position in one table.", [
    ("Question", "The position", "Chief passages"),
    ("What stands first in the gospel Paul preached?", "That Christ died for our sins, was buried and rose the third day, according to the scriptures", "1Cor 15:3, 4"),
    ("What is the problem in Romans 3?", "The righteousness of God, which needed to be declared", "Rom 3:25, 26"),
    ("What does the Cross do in Romans 5?", "Christ dies for the ungodly; enemies are reconciled", "Rom 5:6–11"),
    ("What is the curse in Galatians 3?", "The curse the law pronounces; Christ is made a curse for us", "Gal 3:10–14"),
    ("What follows from one dying for all?", "All were dead; the living live unto him who died and rose", "2Cor 5:14, 15"),
    ("What is said of the believer and the Cross?", "He died with Christ; he is to reckon himself dead unto sin", "Rom 6:6, 11; Col 3:3"),
    ("What is the record in Colossians 2?", "A handwriting of ordinances, blotted out and nailed to the cross", "Col 2:14, 15"),
    ("What do the blood and the cross make in Ephesians and Colossians?", "Nearness, peace, redemption and forgiveness", "Eph 2:13–16; Col 1:14, 20"),
    ("Does one passage explain the others?", "No; each is complete in its passage", "Chapters 13 to 15"),
  ], [0.30, 0.46, 0.24]),
("p", "The volume asks to be tested. A reader who disagrees may take any row of the table and look for the verse that overturns it. If the verse is there, the position must change. If it is not, the position stands on what the text says. In either case the reader will have read the passages on the Cross with the right question in hand, which is what each of them was written to answer."),
("p", "The volume has tried to be fair to the readings it opposes. Thomas Constable sees, in the passages he comments on, the substitution, the sacrifice, the record and the triumph, and he is right to see them. Where he adds an extent or an identification that the sentences do not state, the volume has declined to add it and has said where it parts company. The One of whom all the passages speak is the Lord Jesus Christ, who “gave himself for our sins” (Galatians 1:4), and the reader who comes away holding the sentences more closely has gained what the volume set out to give."),
]

